<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kudur-Mabuk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">19th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century-bce">19th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kudur-Mabuk was an Amorite chief of the Yamutbal tribe in the region east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, whose name is Elamite. Around 1834 BCE he took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a> and installed his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warad-sin">Warad-Sin</a> as king, followed by another son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rim-sin-i">Rim-Sin I</a>, while he himself kept the title of 'father of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amorites">Amorites</a>'. His sons made Larsa the leading power of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> until its fall to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kudur-mabuk.jpg" alt="Kudur-Mabuk" width="320" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jastrow · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Foundation_tablet_Nanaia_Louvre_AO4412_(perspective).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
